    "competitive_moat": {
     "indicator": "competitive_moat",
     "score": 5,
     "confidence": "M",
     "rationale": "Clears all four rubric cross-checks with real, sourced evidence. (a) Pricing power: March 2026 price hike (Premium to $26.99/mo) with JPMorgan/Citi projecting ~$1.7B annualized revenue lift -- pricing power without volume loss, by analyst consensus. (b) Margin vs. peers: 2026 operating margin guided to 31.5% (up from 29.5% in 2025, itself +3pp YoY) -- the highest operating margin of any major pure-play streaming service, per the company's own Q4 2025 shareholder letter (content amortization growing ~10% vs. revenue growth of 12-14%). (c) Retention/switching costs: the password-sharing crackdown (global by end-2024) added 23M+ net subscribers over the following year against a projected 10-20% household churn; resubscribe rates among affected households now exceed 40% -- real, tested evidence of low switching despite an actively imposed friction. (d) Market share: 325M+ global subscribers, more than double Disney+ (~132M), with average U.S. watch time over 1hr/day vs. 36min for the next-closest competitor (Hulu). Scored 5 (wide moat, multiple reinforcing advantages) at Medium rather than High confidence because the market-share and retention evidence, while consistent across sources, comes from independent research/analytics writeups rather than audited filings -- only the margin data is primary-source (company shareholder letter).",
     "source": "manual",
     "citations": [],
     "model": null,
     "prompt_hash": null,
     "proposed_at": "2026-08-24T16:49:50+00:00"
    },
    "revenue_visibility": {
     "indicator": "revenue_visibility",
     "score": 4,
     "confidence": "M",
     "rationale": "Revenue is essentially all subscription-based with no meaningful separate licensing/wholesale line (unlike DIS). Not literally \"contracted\" -- subscriptions are month-to-month, cancel-anytime -- so not scored a 5. But practical visibility is high: the ability of JPMorgan/Citi to project a precise ~$1.7B revenue lift from a specific March 2026 price change implies a highly stable, low-churn, predictable subscriber base in aggregate. Scored 4 (60-80% band) as the better fit than a literal-contracts reading would suggest, given the rubric explicitly allows \"recurring\" alongside \"contracted\" revenue. This is a judgment call on how strictly to read \"contracted\" for a subscription (vs. contract-locked) business -- worth Alan's explicit view before finalizing. Sourced from Netflix Q4 2025 shareholder letter (company IR) and 2026 price-increase analyst coverage (CNBC, JPMorgan/Citi via FinancialContent).",
     "source": "manual",
